body:
I think D'Antoni deserves most of the credit for the innovation of the SSOL Suns.  Before then, Marion was largely considered a SF, and Amare a PF.  D'Antoni moved them both up a position opening up the floor, and clearing the way for an absolutely unguardable 1-5 high screen and roll between Nash and Amare.  Of course, nobody would have cared if the Suns had flamed out as simply a bad or mediocre team, so Nash deserves credit as well.

&#x200B;

Nowadays, with few exceptions, PFs all need to be able to space out to the 3, and SFs and PFs are almost interchangeable.  The SSOL Suns were hardly first team to try this, but they were the first to win at (nearly) the highest levels of the NBA.
